The assertions at the end of the method body could be replaced to ensure that the property you've selected to observe holds. However, it's important to note that, in the context of unit tests, assertions are generally used to ensure that a condition holds true. In our scenario, we are trying to ensure that the property holds in the post-state. But it's hard for us to predict what the post-state of the ArrayList's properties would be, hence the absence of assertions. In the context of a unit test, the verification of the postcondition might be done separately by testing another method that uses the ArrayList and ensures the postcondition.


